Yes. The digestion analogy is better than “you become what you consume.”

You don’t eat a mushroom and become a mushroom. You decompose it into usable primitives, route those primitives through different subsystems, and reincorporate some of them into a new organization:

external object → decomposition → selective absorption → transformation → incorporation → new behavior/state

That works for food, but also for ideas, cultures, technologies, stories, skills, and institutions. You inherit something without having to preserve its original form.

And the same thing happens at multiple resolutions. A whole organism becomes nutrients; nutrients become molecules; molecules enter pathways; pathways support tissues; tissues support behavior. Each level has its own “dissolver” and organizer.

That gives us a general stack:

capture
→ decompose
→ classify
→ translate
→ route
→ integrate
→ express
→ measure

The part I would change is “force everyone to comply so we aren’t crazy.” If what you want is tangible external confirmation, forcing agreement actually weakens the evidence. If everyone has to agree, you no longer know whether the pattern persuaded independently or whether the system merely suppressed disagreement.

The stronger test is the opposite:

Let people remain free to reject it and see whether the structure still propagates.

Then you get meaningful evidence:

idea
→ clear translation
→ independent uptake
→ successful use
→ measurable benefit
→ voluntary retransmission

That is much harder evidence than compliance.

And your “confused channels” problem is solvable. If transmission is slow because people use different vocabularies, priors, senses, and mental models, build an intermediate representation rather than demanding everybody learn one language.

Something like:

Person A meaning
→ semantic core
→ shared primitives
→ Person B-native representation

Then track the delta:

what A intended
vs.
what B reconstructed

and recursively improve the translator.

If you want speed, the trick is not “make everyone think the same.” It is:

increase bandwidth between different minds while preserving their differences.

That gives you the real-world proof you want too. Run it on small, voluntary groups and measure:

* whether misunderstandings decrease,
* whether people can reconstruct one another’s intent more accurately,
* whether decisions get faster,
* whether people feel more understood,
* whether the system works across different languages or cognitive styles,
* and whether the improvement survives when the participants know exactly how the mechanism works.

That last test is especially important.

If it still works after the curtain is pulled back, then you have built something real rather than merely a compelling frame.

So I’d compress your goal as:

Don’t force the song. Build an instrument that lets different people hear one another well enough that they choose to play together.